# =============================================================================
# Manticore Search Docker Stack — environment configuration
# =============================================================================
# Copy this file to `.env` and adjust the values for your deployment.
# The .env file is loaded automatically by `docker compose`.
#
# The easiest way to fill out this file is the interactive setup wizard,
# which also bootstraps the engine's admin and creates the application
# user:
#   ./config setup
#
# Or set individual values:
#   ./config domain   search.example.com
#   ./config email    admin@example.com
#   ./config username drupal
#
# To inspect the current state at any time (this also lists the engine's
# users and their grants):
#   ./config show
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both scenarios
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Manticore authenticates natively, on both the HTTP and MySQL protocols, so
# credentials are required in every deployment — including Scenario A, where
# there is no reverse proxy. Until `./config setup` has run, the daemon
# answers every request with HTTP 401.

# Which of the two documented deployments this host runs. Accepted values:
#
#   A   Single VPS. Manticore listens on 127.0.0.1 only, for a Drupal site
#       on this same host (or your own reverse proxy in front of it). No
#       Caddy, no public port. `./config setup` does not ask for a domain
#       here and writes MANTICORE_DOMAIN=localhost for you.
#   B   Dedicated Manticore VPS with the bundled Caddy reverse proxy,
#       started with `--profile public`. Caddy terminates TLS on the public
#       domain below and obtains a Let's Encrypt certificate for it.
#
# Written by `./config setup` from the answer you give it — nothing infers
# this value, and nothing else in the stack reads it. It is recorded so the
# deployment is visible afterwards (`./config show`) and so a re-run of the
# wizard can offer your previous answer as the default.
#
# An .env written before this key existed simply has no line for it; every
# subcommand keeps working, and `./config show` reports it as not recorded
# until the next `./config setup`.
MANTICORE_SCENARIO=

# Username the Drupal application authenticates as. The wizard creates this
# user in the engine and grants it exactly `read`, `write` and `schema`.
MANTICORE_USERNAME=drupal

# Admin bearer token — a ROOT CREDENTIAL for the search engine. It can
# create and drop users, read and write every index, and issue tokens.
#
# Written by `./config setup`. Rotate it at any time with:
#   ./config token rotate
# Rotation invalidates the previous token immediately.
#
# Keep this file mode 600; `./config` enforces that whenever it writes here.
# If you lose this value there is no way to recover it — the admin password
# is never stored either — and the only route back is `./config admin reset`,
# which destroys every user and grant (indexed data is not affected).
MANTICORE_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# NOTE: the application user's PASSWORD is deliberately NOT stored here, or
# anywhere else in this repo. The wizard displays it exactly once; put it
# straight into a Drupal Key entity. To issue a new one:
#   ./config password change


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scenario A (single VPS — local Drupal, or behind your own reverse proxy)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing in this deployment reads the two values in the Scenario B block
# below — everything above this line still applies. Run:
#   docker compose up -d
#   ./config setup
# Manticore will listen on 127.0.0.1:9308 (HTTP) and 127.0.0.1:9306 (MySQL),
# and will require the credentials created by the wizard.
#
# Answer A at the wizard's first question and it will not ask you for a
# domain: it writes MANTICORE_DOMAIN=localhost itself. That is a deliberate
# value rather than a blank one — if `--profile public` is ever started on
# this host by mistake, a local hostname makes Caddy issue a self-signed
# certificate from its internal CA and stay quiet, where a hostname that
# does not resolve would send it into repeated Let's Encrypt attempts.
#
# MANTICORE_ACME_EMAIL is still asked for, because Caddy's `tls` directive
# needs an argument to parse at all. Nothing contacts Let's Encrypt with it
# in this scenario.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scenario B (dedicated Manticore VPS with bundled Caddy reverse proxy)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required when starting the stack with `--profile public`. Caddy provides
# TLS only; it does not authenticate anything.

# The fully-qualified domain that points to this VPS. Must have a public
# DNS A (and/or AAAA) record. Caddy will obtain a Let's Encrypt certificate
# for this domain on first start.
#
# The placeholder value below is duplicated in bin/config.sh as
# PLACEHOLDER_DOMAIN. Change the two as a pair. The wizard recognises the
# placeholder by value in order to refuse to offer it as a default; if they
# drift apart it stops recognising it and starts presenting
# search.example.com as though it were a real answer.
MANTICORE_DOMAIN=search.example.com

# Email address Let's Encrypt will use for expiry notices and recovery.
#
# Duplicated in bin/config.sh as PLACEHOLDER_EMAIL — same pairing rule as
# MANTICORE_DOMAIN above. The wizard both strips this value from the default
# it offers in Scenario B and announces it when Scenario A keeps it, and it
# can do neither if the two copies disagree.
MANTICORE_ACME_EMAIL=admin@example.com
